l-dokploy-smoke): (1) `SINGLE-PACKAGE INTERNAL TEST REUSING THE EXISTING TENANT-CONSTANT FIXTURES — NO NEW STORE / FAKE / RECORDER NEEDED` — the canonical pair lives in `package policy` (the SAME internal test package as the existing `policy_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `orgA` / `orgB` constants, the `principalIn` / `resourceIn` / `assertDecision` helpers, and the package-private `builtinRoleCaps` matrix verbatim. Unlike BE-0395..BE-0397 (which each introduced a new in-file concurrent fixture for the burst member because the chokepoint had observable mutable state), policy.Engine.Decide is a documented pure function with no I/O and no shared mutable state; the contention burst therefore needs no fixture at all — every goroutine constructs its own (Principal, Action, Resource) tuple from the closed-set coverage table and asserts the verdict matches the table's prediction for ITS OWN tuple. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a pure function: skip the concurrent-fixture pattern entirely; let the goroutines share the immutable Engine and assert per-iteration verdict-vs-prediction equality. (2) `THE COVERAGE TABLE AND THE BURST PREDICTION SHARE ONE SOURCE-OF-TRUTH FUNCTION` — buildTenantIsolationScenarios(t) returns a []tenantIsolationScenario where each entry pairs the input tuple with the predicted (wantAllow, wantReason). The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests; the burst member draws from the SAME table (indexed by `(w * iters + i) % len(scenarios)`) and asserts the engine's runtime verdict matches `scen.wantAllow / scen.wantReason` for the SPECIFIC tuple THIS goroutine drew. A cross-write under the race that swapped two goroutines' tuples would yield a verdict that matches the OTHER goroutine's prediction (not THIS one's), and the per-iteration assertion would fail with the offending scenario name. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set scenario table feeds both a cover